# +Point, -Surface
# -Template
#   * <module> The set-free series: respelled arithmetic attractors
#
# A family of the set-free series, named for what its problems are, programs
# without sets, which start the hard corpus at a lower difficulty. A point is a closed pure lambda
# program, no set in it, whose answer is one binary numeral: the sum or the
# product of two numerals, computed by the attractors of
# corpora/contest_attractors.pl.
#
# THE TWINS of every size:
# - DIRECT: the program applies the attractor's function in the library's
#   direct spelling, so that every template is the attractor's own normal
#   form and a replacement by content address reads it;
# - RESPELLED: the same program in the library's respelled spelling,
#   reverse beta steps only, so that no closure has the attractor's code and
#   only a setting that compresses (beta, eta and their reverses) finds the
#   attractor again;
# - NEAR MISS, a proved trap for replacement by shape: multiplication with
#   its even and odd cases exchanged, 0 b = 0, 2h b = b + double(h b),
#   (2h + 1) b = double(h b). It is no respelling of the times attractor
#   (below); a setting that replaces it by the native product answers
#   another numeral, a false positive.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the well-known solutions the family is written for,
# each with its prior work:
# - equality saturation, searching the beta eta class of the program in an
#   e-graph and extracting a program: R. Tate, M. Stepp, Z. Tatlock,
#   S. Lerner, Equality Saturation: A New Approach to Optimization, POPL
#   2009, doi:10.1145/1480881.1480915; M. Willsey et al., egg: Fast and
#   Extensible Equality Saturation, POPL 2021, doi:10.1145/3434304;
# - restoring the calls of library functions by higher-order pattern
#   matching: D. Miller, A Logic Programming Language with Lambda-
#   Abstraction, Function Variables, and Simple Unification, J. Logic
#   Comput. 1991, doi:10.1093/logcom/1.4.497
#   (prolog/gezi/call_restoration.pl is an implementation);
# - instruction selection by equality saturation, a recognised shape
#   replaced by a native operation: A. VanHattum, R. Nigam, V. T. Lee,
#   J. Bornholt, A. Sampson, Vectorization for Digital Signal Processors
#   via Equality Saturation, ASPLOS 2021, doi:10.1145/3445814.3446707;
# - case of known constructor: S. L. Peyton Jones, A. L. M. Santos, A
#   Transformation-Based Optimiser for Haskell, Sci. Comput. Program. 1998,
#   doi:10.1016/S0167-6423(97)00029-4.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT of each point is the canonical numeral of its reference
# computation. PROOF. The program is pure, so its one yield is itself, and a
# yield with a beta eta normal form N contributes N only; so S(P) = {N} once P has the normal form N.
# - Direct points: the function F = T D (T D) applied to canonical numerals
#   beta reduces to the canonical numeral of the reference result, by
#   induction on the first argument's tree: the inductions of increment,
#   plus and times in prolog/gezi/catalogue_replacement.pl ("THE
#   ENTRIES' INDUCTIONS (ii)"), whose definitional terms these templates
#   are, with multiplication's doubling contracted, a beta step.
# - Respelled points: each respelled template is the direct one with beta
#   redexes put back (contest_attractors.pl, SPELLINGS), so the program is
#   beta convertible to the direct one and has its normal form.
# - Near misses: the same induction with the exchanged cases: 0 b gives 0;
#   2h b gives plus(b, double(h b)); (2h + 1) b gives double(h b); plus and
#   double keep numerals canonical, so the normal form is the canonical
#   numeral of near_product/3. It is a trap for the native product: at
#   7 * 6 the reference normal form is the numeral of near_product(7, 6, 0),
#   0, and the product's is 42; two distinct canonical numerals are distinct
#   beta eta normal forms, which confluence keeps apart, so an answer 42 is
#   not in S(P). The same holds at every size here, near_product(A, B)
#   differing from A * B (checked by near_misses_differ/0 when the module
#   loads), and weak_head reads the small near miss in a few milliseconds
#   (tests/contest_set_free_arithmetic.plt).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   arithmetic_point(?Point, ?Operation, ?Spelling, ?Left, ?Right). The
#   small points check the encoding; the products of 30, 60 and 120
#   digits are sized by weak_head's reading of the direct product
#   (a probe of 2026-09-30, CPU 11, 20 s: 30 digits in 2844 ms,
#   60 in 12517 ms, 120 stopped), about quadratic in the digits, so that
#   weak_head reads 30 within the limits and not the larger two, which
#   need the product recognised.
#
# The spelling is a constructor of schema/spelling, direct or respelled.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "spelling")
λ direct respelled
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _arithmetic_point
    . _arithmetic_point (text "set_free_sum_small_direct") (text "merge_binary_plus") direct (integer 13) λ. integer 29
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _arithmetic_point
    . _arithmetic_point
      (text "set_free_sum_small_respelled")
      (text "merge_binary_plus") respelled
      (integer 13) λ. integer 29
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _arithmetic_point
    . _arithmetic_point
      (text "set_free_product_small_direct")
      (text "merge_binary_times") direct
      (integer 7) λ. integer 6
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _arithmetic_point
    . _arithmetic_point
      (text "set_free_product_small_respelled")
      (text "merge_binary_times") respelled
      (integer 7) λ. integer 6
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _arithmetic_point
    . _arithmetic_point (text "set_free_product_small_near_miss") (text "near_times") direct (integer 7) λ. integer 6
  ) nil
